    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thx. can u expand a bit more on the universalist side though? i feel like the arguments for keeping stuff in big museums are often dismissed as being just about \"colonialism,\" but there's more to it than that, right? what are the actual, core justifications for why these big institutions think it's better for the world to keep these collections together?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core justification for the universalist side is the argument for **global comparative study and robust preservation standards**. Proponents argue that a highly specialized, world-class institution can provide the best-in-class state-of-the-art conservation and deep academic support that a less-resourced local institution might not be able to guarantee the long term.\n\nFurthermore, the \"universal view\" suggests that the *best* way to understand a global artifact is to see it within the context of a world-class comparative collection, allowing for complex, cross-cultural research that might not happen in a more politically focused, single-nation context.",
      "source": "target",
